WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest is the lea
political correspondent

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
for "The Atlantic"
and the author of the new book

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Battle for the Soul:
Inside the Democrats' Campaign

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
to Defeat Trump,"
which is available now.

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:12.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome to the show
Edward-Isaac Dovere.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
How are you?
-Hey, Seth. How are you?

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:15.415 size:81% position:41%
-It's nice to see you.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:17.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I actually have been
interviewed by you in the past

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:19.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a podcast,
so it's very nice for me

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be able to
turn the tables on you.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
I see your book behind you
but you also have --

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think this is correct --
are those the campaign buttons

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:31.097
for all 26 Democratic candidates
in this last election?

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
-All 26 of them.
It was --

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.735
You know, I was traveling around
for this campaign.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:39.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
All over the place -- 29 state
is what I ended up doing.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
Iowa, New Hampshire,
all these places.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:43.376
And the way that I tried to keep
my sanity was I was like,

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm gonna make a collection
of these buttons.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:46.913 line:92%
And so there they all are.

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:49.616 size:84% position:42%
You can see the top one
is Marianne Williamson.

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's the craziest one.
It's her face.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:54.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
Most of them are a little bi
more standard than that,

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:56.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it builds up.
I don't know if you can quit

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:58.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
see it in the shot
behind those flowers,

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:01.227 size:81% position:41%
but there's a ticket
from the inauguration,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the Biden-Harris
inauguration.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
I felt like that was -- this i
what it was all leading to

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:06.299 line:92%
so that's where it all goes to

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:08.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You chose to write this boo
in real time

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:12.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
as you covered the campaigns
as opposed to waiting

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:13.907 line:92%
until it was over to write it.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.109 size:75% position:38%
What made you decide
to do it that way?

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:19.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, I decided
to write this book in 2018

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:21.014 line:92%
We sold the book in 2018.

00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:23.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it said in the proposa
for the book

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:24.951 align:start size:94% position:6%
that publishers
were interested in,

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.020 size:78% position:39%
"This is going to be
the craziest election

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has ever happened
and probably

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.491
the most important electio
in the history of America.

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:34.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew that
that was the case already

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
without knowing obviously
anything about the pandemic,

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
anything about what happened
after George Floyd,

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:41.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
the economic crisis,
the democracy crisis we're in.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:45.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the proposal got more tru
after I sold it,

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:48.641
but it was already clear
that the existential crisi

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.311
the Democrats were going through
once Trump won,

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.980 size:81% position:41%
it's just such a crazy
collection of things

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:54.647 size:75% position:38% line:92%
that were happening.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:57.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I could see --
26 buttons up there.

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought there would be max
16 people running.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:03.022
But I could tell that these were
really interesting people,

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.658
and to get into these characters
and use them as way to tel

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:07.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
the story of what was going on
with the Democrats

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:11.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
was really interesting to me
and it made it so that

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:12.999 size:75% position:38%
I didn't sleep a lot
over the last year

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:16.569 size:84% position:42%
trying to do my day job
and writing this book,

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.237 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
but there it is.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:18.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think one of
the other things that people

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.640
wouldn't have predicted,
based on the early debates

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.513
is that we would end up with a
successful Biden-Harris ticket

00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:28.815 line:92%
You write a lot about how

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:32.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
they had some contentious
moments on stage.

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:35.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you surprised
at how it resolved itself?

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, you go back
to that first primary debate

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:38.825 line:92%
where she went after him.

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:41.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
Said, "I believe
you're not a racist,"

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:44.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then really
just clotheslined him

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.232 line:92%
about his record on busing

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then the Biden campaig
was enraged by this.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I report in the book
that Biden standing on stage

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
was so annoyed
about what happened

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he turns to Pete Buttigie
standing next to him,

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.043
and he says, "Well, that was
some [bleep] bull [bleep].

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Buttigieg doesn't really
know Biden at that point.

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like, "Whoa!"
It's Pete Buttigieg,

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
who you've met.
Right?

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:06.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
It really hit him.
And the rage was so much

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:10.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
over this that
a couple weeks later,

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:13.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was myself talking to Bide
outside of an event

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he was doing
in Dearborn, Michigan,

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:16.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he was like,
"Oh, that's really nice,

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
not calling me a racist."
But what I got at in the boo

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.635
that I had not known until I
started reporting for the book

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that Jill Biden was
on a phone call

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:27.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
with some supporters
a little bit after the debate,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she was so mad.
You forget sometimes,

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
Jill Biden,
in these elegant dresses,

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:34.047 size:84% position:42%
underneath is, like,
the Philly girl, right?

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she said, you know,
"With the life that he's led

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:37.984 align:start size:94% position:6%
the work that he's done,
to stand up there

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:40.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you call him a racist?
Go [bleep] yourself."

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.455 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, they -- You know,
for people who might not kno

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:48.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
what opposition research
actually means,

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you write that, you know
based on this,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.365 line:92%
Biden went so far as to --

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did he go through
with putting together

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.536 line:92%
opposition research for Kamala

00:03:56.536 --> 00:04:00.907
-Yeah, these are research
that people who go through

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:04.677
tax records and every possible
public thing and private thing

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:08.314
a person's ever done with th
intention of finding bad stuff

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.916 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the Biden campaign
had not --

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
we can forget about it,

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:13.519
given all that's happened
over the last two years,

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
but this time, two years ago
the Biden campaign

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:18.925 size:84% position:42%
was not in great shape,
and they did not think

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
to get a full
opposition research

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
about all of their rivals,
including Harris.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.934
When this happens, they say,
"Uh-oh," and they right away

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
order from
an opposition researcher

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
who had already
prepared something.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
Two days after the debate,
they say, "All right.

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:35.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're gonna need that,"
and paid a lot of money for it

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:38.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The book ends with you having
a conversation

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
with President Biden
but obviously not in person,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is how
most of this reporting

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.584 line:92%
would happen in a different era.

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:48.354
What was the timeline of whe
you were speaking with him

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.423 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and what was that like?

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:53.393
-Well, so it's crazy, right?
It was COVID restrictions.

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
I spent a lot of time
covering Biden.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's a very
hands-on person, right?

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.199 line:92%
But we were over the phone

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we were on the phone
February 2nd.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.938
The interesting thing about that
is that it was both

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
his late son Beau's birthday
as it happened,

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:11.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was right before
he signed the executive orders

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
that got rid of
the Trump executive orders

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
So at the end of the
conversation, he said to me,

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:18.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I got to go. I've got to sign
these executive orders.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is important."
I said, "That's fair."

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.822 line:92%
But when we got on the phone

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was a little bit
of chitchat,

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:25.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I said to him,
"So, what's it like?

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:28.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you gotten used
to being in the Oval Offic

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:30.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
and waking up upstairs?"
And he said to me,

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:32.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You know, the Oval Office
I've been here a lot.

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:36.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was here almost every da
for eight years, but upstairs,

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.972 size:81% position:41%
you know, I sometimes
can't find my clothes.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:42.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nobody told me that moving
was part of this, man."

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:44.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
And I said to him --

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:46.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I don't know,
it's the way I am

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:47.980 align:start size:94% position:6%
in interviews with people.
I said,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.182 size:78% position:39%
"Well, you're the one
who wanted this job."

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:51.751
And he said to me, "You know
someone said to me I'm lik

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:54.887
the dog that caught the ca
after running for so long.

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.723 line:92%
He says, "No, I caught the bus

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:57.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm the dog
that caught the bus."

00:05:57.757 --> 00:06:01.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
You also talk about, you know,

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:05.598
that there was this existentia
crisis with the Democratic Party

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:07.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
after 2016.
Obviously it caught

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:10.303
not just them but I think
a lot of people off guard.

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:13.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like the party
knows what it is now,

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you feel like
they won this election by,

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:20.980 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, a hair's breadth
and they'll be --

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
the same thing
will happen in the future?

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:24.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I don't think
the same thing will happen

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:27.220
but I don't think it's done.
It's not like the book ends,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:27.920 line:92%
and it's like, "The end,"

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
and everybody rides
into the sunset or whatever.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.725
The best way that I guess
I could describe it is,

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:35.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's like the end of
the first "Star Wars" movie.

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:38.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm not a real
"Star Wars" aficionado,

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the Death Star
gets destroyed,

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:40.967 size:84% position:42% line:92%
but that's not the end.

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:43.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a --
The Empire is still there.

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:45.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's another Death Star
that gets built.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
Part of what's going on
in this book is explaining

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:49.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
what happened
over these four years,

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:54.280
all the things that were going
on in the Democratic turmoil

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.715 line:92%
And some of it's resolved,

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:57.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some of it is not resolved
at the end.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, of course,
Biden and the Democrats

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:02.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
didn't do as well
as they thought they might

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
They thought that they wer
gonna be in a clear win

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the Senate and that
they weren't going to lose

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:09.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
as many House seats as they di
in November,

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:11.998
and that in itself is a bi
problem for the Democrats

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:13.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
and looking
what's going forward.

00:07:13.032 --> 00:07:15.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, of course,
trying to figure out socialism

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:18.004
moderates, progressivism,
what does Biden believe,

00:07:18.004 --> 00:07:21.374
how do any of these things work,
and all of it with the midterm

00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:23.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
already starting
to stare down at people.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:25.878
-Also, I'd like to take a second
to say, it's still shockin

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:29.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
to me that the best "Star Wars
could do was come up

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:31.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a bigger Death Star.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:33.286
-You know, you should take
that up with George Lucas.

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:37.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's not for me, Seth.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.657 --> 00:07:39.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:40.526 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Congrats on the book.

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:42.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's really nice
talking to you again.

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:43.496 line:92%
-Thanks, Seth, I appreciate it

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:46.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Battle for the Soul:
Inside the Democrats' Campaign

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:48.668
To Defeat Trump" is availabl
now wherever books are sold,

00:07:48.668 --> 00:07:52.538
but please support your loca
